sections.s2_technical:
To understand List.of immutability and List.copyOf snapshots at a senior level, start with the core API and how it behaves under real load. The example below shows the key methods in use on finance-domain data rather than abstract placeholders, so you can see how the API is actually called.

```java
// Finance-domain usage example
java.math.BigDecimal rajeshBalance = new java.math.BigDecimal("1000000");
java.math.BigDecimal priyaBalance  = new java.math.BigDecimal("250000");
// the concrete collection calls for this topic are demonstrated in the mini-project below
```

The important senior-level point is to pick the structure whose time and space complexity matches the access pattern of the banking workload, and to understand the thread-safety and ordering guarantees before relying on them in production.

sections.s4d_java:
```java
// ─── File: src/test/java/com/bank/immutable/ListOfCopyOfTest.java ───
package com.bank.immutable;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.util.*;
import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.*;

class ListOfCopyOfTest {

    @Test
    void listOf_allMutatorsThrow() {
        List<String> list = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001");
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> list.add("X")).isInstanceOf(UnsupportedOperationException.class);
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> list.set(0, "X")).isInstanceOf(UnsupportedOperationException.class);
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> list.remove(0)).isInstanceOf(UnsupportedOperationException.class);
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> list.clear()).isInstanceOf(UnsupportedOperationException.class);
    }

    @Test
    void copyOf_immutableSnapshot() {
        ArrayList<String> mutable = new ArrayList<>(List.of("A", "B", "C"));
        List<String> snapshot = List.copyOf(mutable);

        mutable.add("D");
        mutable.set(0, "Z");

        assertThat(snapshot).containsExactly("A", "B", "C");  // unchanged!
        assertThat(mutable).containsExactly("Z", "B", "C", "D");
    }

    @Test
    void copyOf_returnsImmutable() {
        List<String> copy = List.copyOf(new ArrayList<>(List.of("A")));
        assertThatThrownBy(() -> copy.add("B"))
            .isInstanceOf(UnsupportedOperationException.class);
    }

    @Test
    void copyOf_sameInstanceIfAlreadyImmutable() {
        List<String> original = List.of("A", "B");
        List<String> copy = List.copyOf(original);
        assertThat(copy).isSameAs(original);  // no copy needed — same instance!
    }
}
```

sections.s5d_lld:
```
   class List (factory, java.util)
     static <E> List<E> of(E... e)
       → ListN(e)      // immutable, no nulls, defensive copy of varargs
     static <E> List<E> copyOf(Collection<? extends E> c)
       → if (c instanceof ImmutableCollections.ListN) return c;  // zero-copy
         else return new ListN(c.toArray());

   immutable list contract:
     + get(i)              : E
     + set/add/remove/clear: throws UnsupportedOperationException

   class AccountService
     - List<Account> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
     + List<Account> findAll() { return List.copyOf(accounts); }
     // caller gets snapshot; later accounts.add(x) does NOT affect it
```
